// draft
// drafted
type Arg = { iterable: Bool, value: Text, items: Text[] }

value_chain(args: Arg[]) -> Text[]
	note "emit each argument in order: an iterable argument's items one level deep, a non-iterable argument (strings included) as its value"
	# The output is drawn from the arguments' contributions, where a non-iterable argument
	# contributes its value and an iterable argument contributes its items.
	// reaching for: out drawn_from the list made of each non-iterable argument's value and each
	// iterable argument's items, as a multiset. `flatten(args.items)` also takes the items field of
	// non-iterable arguments, and the notation has no filtered list or per-element choice of field
	// to build the right list. `all x in out: any a in args: ...` would keep membership but lose
	// multiplicity, which widens the sentence.
	# The output has as many elements as there are non-iterable arguments plus the items of the
	# iterable arguments together.
	// reaching for: len(out) == (the number of arguments with iterable false) + (the sum of
	// len(a.items) over the arguments with iterable true). The notation has no count or sum over a
	// filtered list, and `len(flatten(args.items))` would also count the ignored items of
	// non-iterable arguments.
	# Everything an earlier argument contributes comes before everything a later argument
	# contributes.
	// reaching for: for arguments a before b in args, every output occurrence that a contributes
	// comes before every output occurrence that b contributes. `x before y in out` on contributed
	// values resolves to first occurrences in out, so the same value contributed twice (the wanted
	// example) makes the line false on the correct output. The notation also cannot choose a
	// contribution by case (a value or the items) inside one quantifier.
	# An iterable argument's items keep their order in the output.
	all a in args where a.iterable: a.items same_order_as out
	// example wanted: no arguments at all; nothing is contributed
	// example wanted: an iterable argument with no items between two non-iterable ones; the empty
	// argument contributes nothing and the neighbours stay in order
	// example wanted: text arguments "12" and "34" passed non-iterable, then an iterable of "56"
	// and "78"; strings are emitted as-is, not split into characters
	// example wanted: a non-iterable 1 followed by an iterable of 2 through 6, and the mirror case,
	// an iterable of 1 through 5 followed by a non-iterable 6; prepend and append
	// example wanted: an iterable argument whose item is itself a nested list, written as the text
	// "[7, 8]"; one level only, so the item is emitted whole
	// example wanted: the same value passed twice, once as a non-iterable argument and once as an
	// item; both occurrences are kept
	// example wanted: a non-iterable argument whose items field is non-empty, and an iterable one
	// whose value is non-empty; the ignored field contributes nothing
